ossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Recommender systems are central to online platforms but remain di!cult to deploy in o\"ine retail, where the closed-loop behavioral data infrastructure underlying conventional recommenders is largely absent. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) create an opportunity to build recommendation around the natural interaction logic of o\"ine retail, in which customers express their needs directly through language. Realizing this opportunity, however, requires translating open-ended customer requests into accurate product rankings and enabling the recommender to improve across deployment stages. We address these challenges with O#RecLLM, a two-phase LLM-based recommendation framework. The first phase constructs customer requests associated with purchases from retailers' existing data and learns a deployable foundation recommender that first identifies broadly relevant products, then narrows this pool to a smaller consideration set, and finally performs fine-grained residual reranking. The second phase continues learning from authentic customer requests and preference feedback observed after deployment, converting these signals into pairwise preferences and applying direct preference optimization from customer feedback (DPO-CF) while remaining anchored to the foundation recommender. We evaluate O#RecLLM with a large toy retail chain in Asia. The foundation recommender substantially outperforms both structured and LLM-based recommendation benchmarks and 60 experienced sales associates in a source-blind randomized customer experiment. Using customers' authentic feedback from this experiment, DPO-CF produces a preference-optimized recommender that further improves performance in a second randomized customer experiment. Together, these results establish O#RecLLM as a general framework for developing LLM-based recommenders in o\"ine retail, from initial deployment with existing retailer data to continuous improvement through subsequent customer interactions.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7428218","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]],"date-time":"2026-09-08T17:15:12Z","timestamp":1788887712000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["LLM for Offline Retail Recommendation"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-3788-8786","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Xincheng (Max)","family":"Ma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qi","family":"Qi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yicheng","family":"Song","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tianshu","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","reference":[{"key":"ref1","author":"M Andr\ufffde","year":"2025","journal-title":"Should retailers close stores or make them work harder? EY Global Retail Insights"},{"key":"ref2","first-page":"785","article-title":"XGBoost: A scalable tree boosting system","author":"T Chen","year":"2016","journal-title":"Proceedings of the 22nd ACM SIGKDD International Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining (KDD)"},{"key":"ref3","author":"Deepseek-Ai","year":"2024","journal-title":"DeepSeek LLM: Scaling open-source language models with longtermism"},{"key":"ref4","first-page":"4171","article-title":"BERT: Pre-training of deep bidirectional transformers for language understanding","author":"J Devlin","year":"2019","journal-title":"Proceedings of the 2019 NAACL-HLT"},{"key":"ref5","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"299","DOI":"10.1145\/3523227.3546767","author":"S Geng","year":"2022","journal-title":"Proceedings of the 16th ACM Conference on Recommender Systems"},{"issue":"11","key":"ref6","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"5027","DOI":"10.1287\/mn